# Fix PWA Service Worker Registration Issue

The root cause of the "PWA app not loading" issue is a race condition in the [PwaRegister.tsx](file:///d:/Institute/BCE/src/components/PwaRegister.tsx) component. The component wraps the `navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js')` call inside a `window.addEventListener('load', ...)` callback. In modern Next.js applications using the App Router, the [PwaRegister](file:///d:/Institute/BCE/src/components/PwaRegister.tsx#5-18) component often hydrates and executes its `useEffect` *after* the `window.onload` event has already fired. Consequently, the load event listener is never triggered, the Service Worker is never registered, and the PWA fails to initialize.

## Proposed Changes

We will modify the [PwaRegister.tsx](file:///d:/Institute/BCE/src/components/PwaRegister.tsx) component in both projects to execute the registration without relying on the `load` event listener, as the `useEffect` itself already runs on the client after hydration.

### Project BCE (Smart Learn)

#### [MODIFY] [PwaRegister.tsx](file:///d:/Institute/BCE/src/components/PwaRegister.tsx)
- Remove `window.addEventListener('load', ...)` wrapper.
- Call `navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js')` directly inside the `useEffect`.

### Project Institute1 (Smart Learn)

#### [MODIFY] [PwaRegister.tsx](file:///d:/Institute/institute1/src/components/PwaRegister.tsx)
- Remove `window.addEventListener('load', ...)` wrapper.
- Call `navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js')` directly inside the `useEffect`.

